{
  "_KIT_CLASS": "KIT \u2014 the opt-in harness wiring, as a shape. This file is JSON and has no comment syntax, so its classification marker is this KEY rather than a leading comment; process/EXTRACTION.md \u00a7 The one file classification convention states the carve-out. There are no values inside it to replace \u2014 \u00a7 2.8 records why, and every row of the table that once listed them is struck.",
  "_README": "Kit template — copy to .claude/settings.json to ACTIVATE the Claude Code hooks (they execute shell commands on tool use / session start, so activation is a deliberate operator decision). The git-level commit-prefix guard (scripts/githooks/commit-msg) is wired separately via `git config core.hooksPath scripts/githooks` — kit-init.sh wires it ONCE at adoption and commits the hooks' executable bit, setup.sh re-wires and repairs it on EVERY fresh clone (process/contracts/initializer.md § 2) — and does NOT depend on this file.",

  "_NO_PLACEHOLDERS": "This file has NO <angle-bracket> tokens to fill in — copy it as it stands. It used to, and a `_PLACEHOLDERS` map here glossed seven of them (<trunk>, <PREFIX>, <code-path-globs>, <metadata-path-globs>, <adapter>, <project-facts>, <role-prefix-list>). Every one of those lived in the `autoMode` block that _WHAT_THIS_FILE_MAY_CONTAIN records as deleted; the map outlived the tokens it described and told an adopter to go replace things that were not here. Those values are real, and they are declared where they are USED — the adapter, scripts/config.sh, and the commit-msg hook — not glossed a second time here. scripts/test/run.sh asserts that any <token> this file glosses actually appears in it.",

  "_WHAT_THIS_FILE_MAY_CONTAIN": "ONLY SETTINGS KEYS WHOSE EFFECT THE KIT CAN DEMONSTRATE. This file used to carry an `autoMode` block (prose `allow`/`soft_deny`/`environment` lists describing direct-to-trunk authorization) and a `plansDirectory` value. Both were removed, and no verification of the host's settings schema was needed to decide it: NOTHING IN THE KIT READ EITHER KEY, the `autoMode` arrays held English sentences rather than tool patterns so no permission engine could enforce them under any key name, and this file's own note says unknown keys are ignored. So it enforced nothing AND guided nobody — a settings file is never read into an agent's context — while reading exactly like active authorization policy, which is the one thing an adopter would believe. The rule generalizes: if the kit cannot show what a key does, the kit does not ship it. Add a key here only alongside the thing that reads it.",

  "_WHERE_THE_TRUNK_POLICY_ACTUALLY_LIVES": "The adapter (CLAUDE.md) § 'The trunk, the branches, and what counts as code here', which cites process/MANUAL.md § The code-vs-metadata rule. Both are documents an agent actually reads. The deleted `autoMode` prose was a THIRD copy of that rule; it is not reproduced here, because a third copy is what drifts.",

  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write|NotebookEdit",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/scripts/hooks/require-role.sh\"" }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/scripts/hooks/session-start.sh\"" }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "Notification": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/scripts/notify-hook.sh\"" }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
